{"name":"Burgundy Snail","hex":"#7e7150","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"burgundy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Burgundy (red wine).","A dark red colour tinged with purple, like that of Burgundy (red) wine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a deep purple red color like that of Burgundy wine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/burgundy"},{"term":"snail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of very many animals (either hermaphroditic or nonhermaphroditic), of the class Gastropoda, having a coiled shell.","A slow person; a sluggard."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or travel very slowly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Helix pomatia","blocks":[{"text":"Helix pomatia, known as the Roman snail, Burgundy snail, or escargot, is a species of large, air-breathing stylommatophoran land snail belonging to the family Helicidae and native to Europe. It is characterized by a globular brown shell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Helix_pomatia_89a.jpg/330px-Helix_pomatia_89a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helix_pomatia","wikidata":"Q245314"},{"title":"Burgundy","blocks":[{"text":"Burgundy is a historical region in France, broadly corresponding to the territory of the former administrative region of the same name, which existed from 1982 to 2015 before it was merged on 1 January 2016 into Bourgogne-Franche-Comté. Its capital is Dijon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Flag_of_Bourgogne.svg/330px-Flag_of_Bourgogne.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burgundy","wikidata":"Q1173"},{"title":"Snail","blocks":[{"text":"A snail is a shelled gastropod. The name is most often applied to land snails, terrestrial pulmonate gastropod molluscs. However, the common name snail is also used for most of the members of the molluscan class Gastropoda that have a coiled shell that is large enough for the animal to retract completely into. When the word \"snail\" is used in this most general sense, it includes not just land snails but also numerous species of sea snails and freshwater snails. Gastropods that naturally lack a shell, or have only an internal shell, are mostly called slugs, and land snails that have only a very small shell are sometimes called semi-slugs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Snail.jpg/330px-Snail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snail","wikidata":"Q308841"},{"title":"Burgundy (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Burgundy is a purplish, dark-red color. The name comes from the Burgundy wine in France. The word \"burgundy\" is not usually capitalized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Danish_Passport_Cover.svg/330px-Danish_Passport_Cover.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burgundy_(color)","wikidata":"Q797446"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png/500px-Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png/500px-Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution map of H. pomatia showing the European countries where the species is present","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia